\section {Introduction}	
The Deep Underground Neutrino Experiment (DUNE) \cite{1} is a next-generation, long-baseline neutrino oscillation experiment with a primary scientific goal of making precise measurements of
the parameters governing long-baseline neutrino oscillation. The prime concern  is the
observation of CP -violation in the neutrino sector. In DUNE, a muon neutrino $(\nu_{\mu} )$- or
muon antineutrino $(\bar{\nu_{\mu}})$ - dominated beam will be produced by the Long-Baseline Neutrino Facility (LBNF) beamline and characterized by a near detector (ND) at Fermilab before
the neutrinos travel 1285 km to the Sanford Underground Research Facility (SURF) in South Dakota, USA. The far detector (FD) will consist of 40 kt (fiducial) liquid argon time projection chamber (LArTPC) detectors. Oscillation probabilities are deduced from comparison of the observed neutrino spectra at the near and far detectors which are used to constrain values of the
neutrino oscillation parameters.

The requirement for precise knowledge of neutrino oscillation  parameters depends on many factors amongst which precise reconstruction of neutrino energy is of utmost importance.As we know that the neutrino oscillation probability itself depends on the energy of the neutrinos, any inaccurate measurement of neutrino energy will be disseminate to the measurements of neutrino oscillation parameters since it causes uncertainties in the cross section measurement and event identification. 

\section{EVENT GENERATORS}
\subsection{GENIE}GENIE \cite{3} is a modern and versatile platform for neutrino event simulation. It has been developed putting special emphasis on scattering in the energy region of few GeV, important for ongoing and future oscillation studies. It is a ROOT \cite{3a} based neutrino event generator designed using object oriented methodologies and developed entirely in C++. GENIE  is  currently  being  used  by  many of neutrino baseline experiments running around the world, such as   Minerva \cite{3b},MINOS \cite{3c},  MicroBooNE \cite{3d} ,NoVA \cite{3e},T2K \cite{3f} , and ArgoNEUT \cite{3g}  experiments.

In GENIE, nuclei  are  modeled  within the Relativistic Fermi gas (RFG)   picture  with  Bodek-Ritchie which has been modified to incorporate short range nucleon-nucleon correlations \cite{3h}. The Llewellyn-Smith model \cite{3i} with the latest BBBA form  factors  parametrization \cite{3j}  is  used  for  quasi-elastcic (QEL)  cross  section  calculations. The default value of axial mass $M_{A}$ considered in CCQE is 0.99 $GeV/c^{2}$. The production of baryon resonances  is included with the Rein-Sehgal \cite{3k} model up  to  the  hadronic  in-variant  mass $W$ =  1.7  GeV. The default value for the resonance axial vector mass $M_{A}$  is 1.12 $GeV/c^{2}$. Deep inelastic scattering (DIS) is calculated in leading order model using the modifications suggested by Bodek and Yang \cite{3l}. In GENIE, DIS is the only mechanism of interaction at $ W>$ 1.7 GeV. For modeling 2p2h interactions, GENIE apply the model proposed by the Valencia group \cite{3m}.

\subsection{NuWro}
The neutrino Monte Carlo generator NuWro has been  developed by a group od physicists  Cezary  Juszczak et al at the Wroclaw University \cite{4a}.Presently NuWro covers neutrino energy range from $\sim 100 $ MeV to $\sim 100 $ GeV. It operates all types of interactions important in neutrino-nucleus interaction as well as DIS hasdronization and intra nuclear cascade. NuWro 
serves as a tool to estimate the relevance of various theoratical models \cite{4b} being investigated currently.

NuWro as so far defined, is arranged around the event structure which contains three vectors of particles: incoming, temporary and outgoing. It holds a structure with all the parameters used and a set of boolean flags labeling the events as quasi-elastic (QEL), resonance excited scattering (RES), deep inelastic (DIS), charged current (CC), neutral current (NC) etc. 

The basic architecture of NuWro follow better known MCs like NEUT \cite{4c} or GENIE \cite{3}. In order to ease comparison, NuWro allows running simulations choosing easily the values of parameters, sets of form-factors, models of nucleus etc. The prominent features of NuWro are: fine hadronization model, description of resonance region without Rein-Sehgal approach \cite{3k} and effective implementation of spectral function in order to describe correctly the distribution of nucleon momenta and binding energies in the impulse approximation scheme.

  



